# Chapter 4, first part: global and local formats, the order in which a
# setting is looked up, and the default format.  Part of
# the chapter source;
# headlines of the second level and below only.

.h2 Global and local formats [format-kinds]
.ix Format, global and local
.ix Global and local formats
.ix Override of formats
.ix Order in which formats are looked up
.ix Resolution order of formats
.ix Format hierarchy

.h3 The two kinds [format-two]
.ix Global setting
.ix Local setting
.ix Text attribute
A global setting is made for the whole document.  It is made on a panel of
the Global menu ({n:format-global-menu}) and is saved with the document.  A
local setting is made for one block, one column of a block, or a run of
characters.  It is saved in the block, the column or the text itself and
overrides the global setting for that piece alone.  The File menu saves,
retrieves and replaces the global settings of a document as a whole
({n:file}).

The settings of the headlines and of the text are separate.  In a structured
document the first block of every section is its headline and the other
blocks are text; the global panels have a Headline and a Text half, and
every level of section from 1 to 5 can have settings of its own.  In an
unstructured document every block is text and no headline setting is used.

.h3 The order in which a setting is looked up [format-order]
.ix Look-up order of a format
.ix Column format
.ix Block format
.ix Named format
When the Editor or the Printer needs the justification, the indent, the
attributes or the tab stops of a piece of text, the first value found in this
order is taken and the search goes no further.
.table 5 16 36
Step | Setting | Where it comes from
1 | Column | The format of the column ({k:F6} in a table).
2 | Block | The format of the block ({k:F6}, {k:Ctrl-H}).
3 | Named format | The named format that the block has.
4 | Level | The Level Format panel, for a headline or text.
5 | Global | The Global Format panel.
6 | Built in | Left, no indent, normal, no tabs (a stop every 5 cells).
.end
The level value is taken only when the block is in a section of level 1 to 5
and the level has a value of its own for the setting; a level that has none
takes the global value.  A headline uses the Headline settings and a text
block the Text settings.  The left block indent is different: it has no
global form.  It is the left block indent of the block, or that of its named
format, and 0 when neither has one.

The font is looked up in the same order ({n:format-set-typefaces}).

.behavior A text attribute is added to the attribute of the format, not put
  in its place.  A block whose format is Bold stays bold when
  {k:Ctrl-N} (Normal) is typed inside it: Normal only switches off what
  the text turned on.  The marks of a run of characters (bold, italics,
  underscore) are kept in the text; the format of the block gives the
  attribute that the text starts with.  Both the screen and the printed page
  work this way.

.h3 The default format [format-default]
.ix Default format
.ix Defaults of the format
.ix New document, format of
.ix Template
The defaults below are what a document has for a setting that nobody has
changed.  They are also what a panel shows for a global setting that has no
value of its own.
.table 28 20
Setting | Default
Headline Attributes | Normal
Headline Justification | Left
Headline Indent | None
Headline Font | Font 4
Headline Ruler | no tab stops
Text Attributes | Normal
Text Justification | Left
Text Indent | None
Text Font | Font 2
Text Ruler | no tab stops
Line Spacing | 1.00
Headline Spacing | 1.50
Block Spacing | 1.50
Subsection Spacing | 1.50
Peer Section Spacing | 2.00
Indent per Level | 0
Auto Numbering? | Yes
.end
A new document does not start with the defaults alone.  It has bold
headlines, evenly justified text, a text ruler with one stop at 5, the
spacings 1.50 for a headline and for a block and 2.00 for a subsection and
a peer section, a level indent of 3, the paper Letter and a title page that
starts 33 % down the page.  Those settings show these values on the
panels and not the defaults of the table.

The defaults of the other panels are in their own sections: Page
({n:format-page}), Typeface ({n:format-typeface}), Marker
({n:format-marker}), Options ({n:format-options}).
